OpenLDAP on openRuyi
===================

Initial configuration
---------------------
The package does not start the server or create a directory database.
Install openldap-client for the LDAP commands below.
Run these commands as root on a new installation:

    slapd-init
    systemctl start slapd
    ldapsearch -Q -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// -b cn=config dn

slapd-init refuses an existing /etc/openldap/slapd.d, including an empty
one. It validates a temporary configuration before it publishes the directory.
Do not delete an existing directory to make initialization succeed.

The service runs as ldap. It accepts local Unix socket connections only.
Only the local root identity can manage cn=config. There is no default
LDAP password, domain, network listener, or business database.
Enable the service with systemctl enable slapd after configuration and tests.

Add a directory database
------------------------
Use ldapadd -Q -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// -f FILE to add configuration entries.
Load the required schema LDIF files from /etc/openldap/schema first.
Do not substitute schema names without checking their attribute definitions.

Load back_mdb.so from @LIBEXECDIR@/openldap with an olcModuleList entry.
Add an olcMdbConfig entry with the suffix, directory, size limit, and ACLs
for your deployment. Use a directory under /var/lib/ldap owned by ldap,
with mode 0700. Give each database its own directory.
Use slappasswd to create a password hash if the database needs a root password.
Do not put a plaintext password in a shell command or use an example password.
See slapd-config(5), slapd-mdb(5), and slapd.access(5).

Manage configuration
--------------------
Use ldapmodify over ldapi:/// for online changes. Do not edit the generated
files in slapd.d or recalculate their CRC values. For offline changes, stop
the service and use slapmodify or export and import with slapcat and slapadd.

For a custom .schema file, use a temporary slapd.conf with the required
include directives. Run slaptest -f FILE -F EMPTY_DIRECTORY to convert it.
Review the generated schema entry before importing it. Keep backups outside
the live configuration directory. See slaptest(8) and slapadd(8).

Network access and TLS
----------------------
Configure certificate, key, and CA paths through cn=config before enabling
network access. The ldap user must be able to read them. The service cannot
read home directories. Use a deployment-owned directory under /etc instead.
Configure access controls and require protected authentication as appropriate.
Test certificate trust and hostname validation from a client.

Use systemctl edit slapd to override the listener list. For example:

    [Service]
    ExecStart=
    ExecStart=@LIBEXECDIR@/slapd -d 0 -F /etc/openldap/slapd.d -h "ldapi:/// ldap://127.0.0.1:389/"
    AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
    CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE

The capability is needed for low TCP ports when the service runs as ldap.
Use ldapsearch -ZZ for StartTLS. Do not disable certificate checks to make
that test pass. Opening a listener does not configure a firewall or TLS.
SASL mechanisms also depend on the installed Cyrus SASL modules. There is
no package-specific mechanism allowlist. Configure GSSAPI only after its
keytab, service identity, and client authentication have been tested.

Existing installations
----------------------
The service no longer reads /etc/sysconfig/openldap or selects slapd.conf.
Review existing listener, identity, and startup options before restarting.
The package does not convert or delete administrator data. A retained old
configuration or .rpmsave file is not used by the new service automatically.
For a legacy slapd.conf, stop the service and convert a copy with slaptest.
Alternatively, use a service override with an explicit -f path while migrating.

Before a 2.6 to 2.7 upgrade, stop slapd and use the 2.6 tools to export
cn=config and every database to separate LDIF files. Save configuration,
certificates, permissions, module lists, and database copies. Check the exports
before replacing the old binaries. The MDB storage format changed in 2.7.

Use the 2.7 tools to import into new, separate directories. Check removed
backends, module paths, ACLs, password policies, and schema before switching.
Validate with slaptest and compare exported entries after import. Keep the old
binaries and untouched data until application tests pass. Do not open a 2.7
MDB directory with 2.6 tools or overwrite the only copy during rollback.

https://www.openldap.org/doc/admin27/appendix-upgrading.html
